WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.909
Welcome to Part 2 of the
implementation series

2
00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:05.988
for Ticket-to-Machine on
ServiceNow and SailPoint.

3
00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:10.001
Part 1 confirmed the boundary,
decided the writers and approvers,

4
00:00:10.001 --> 00:00:12.974
connected the sources,
and opened the front door.

5
00:00:12.974 --> 00:00:16.100
Part 2 covers the three
gates that finish the loop.

6
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.988
First, actuation, where the ticket
authorizes a machine to do the work.

7
00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:24.288
Then the re-read, because a
ticket status isn't proof.

8
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:27.093
And then the tempo, with its measures,

9
00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:30.926
its identity fixes, and
the evidence an ISSO files.

10
00:00:30.926 --> 00:00:34.290
After that, we'll look at the
dangers across all seven gates,

11
00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.725
and what it would take to move
the guide from draft to proved.

12
00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:40.425
It's still an example, not an endorsement.

13
00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:43.754
And because the kit's first
pass doesn't actuate anything,

14
00:00:43.754 --> 00:00:48.010
the first gate here is about
configuring the products themselves.

15
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.298
Here are Part 2's three gates on one page.

16
00:00:54.298 --> 00:00:59.223
Actuation is done when each lane acts
through a connector or a declared exception,

17
00:00:59.223 --> 00:01:03.497
under a service identity, with a change
record where its class needs one.

18
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:07.435
It closes the third gap, and there's
no automated check for it yet.

19
00:01:07.435 --> 00:01:09.723
You'll run one task per lane by hand.

20
00:01:09.723 --> 00:01:13.128
The re-read gate is done
when nothing closes without

21
00:01:13.128 --> 00:01:16.297
a re-read of the target,
which closes the first gap.

22
00:01:16.297 --> 00:01:18.409
The kit's tests prove its logic.

23
00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:21.374
The tempo gate is done when
the windows are measured,

24
00:01:21.374 --> 00:01:26.060
the identity fixes run through the loop,
and the evidence is produced on a schedule.

25
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.272
Two rules run through all three.

26
00:01:28.272 --> 00:01:32.535
A person's role stops at approval,
except for declared exceptions.

27
00:01:32.535 --> 00:01:35.272
And a success response
doesn't count as closure.

28
00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:40.248
The kit's first pass doesn't write to
Entra ID, Active Directory or SailPoint,

29
00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:44.620
which is why the actuation gate
relies on a manual lab for now.

30
00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.857
In the actuation gate, the
ticket authorizes a machine.

31
00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:56.508
For owner-governed access,
SailPoint's connectors do the work:

32
00:01:56.508 --> 00:02:00.520
accounts, groups, roles,
licenses and access packages.

33
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.429
When the Entra connector
disables an account,

34
00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:06.645
it also signs the user out
of any existing sessions.

35
00:02:06.645 --> 00:02:10.353
For low-authority tasks,
ServiceNow's Entra spoke can act,

36
00:02:10.353 --> 00:02:12.720
as long as its actions cover the task.

37
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.307
Check what it really covers.

38
00:02:14.307 --> 00:02:20.658
It has no M F A reset action, and its reset
action works only for cloud-only users.

39
00:02:20.658 --> 00:02:25.164
When a person does have to act, they
activate an eligible role through PIM,

40
00:02:25.164 --> 00:02:27.944
with the ticket number
as the justification.

41
00:02:27.944 --> 00:02:31.108
PIM doesn't check that
number, so a SIEM rule does.

42
00:02:31.108 --> 00:02:33.895
And resetting someone's
authentication methods

43
00:02:33.895 --> 00:02:36.683
stays with the help desk's
identity verification.

44
00:02:36.683 --> 00:02:41.544
A connector's M F A setting is not a
substitute for verifying the person.

45
00:02:41.544 --> 00:02:44.510
The prerequisites are the
front door from gate four,

46
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.600
and the lanes decided in gate two,
already written into the record.

47
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.800
Two more steps cover what
the connectors can't.

48
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.859
SailPoint's service desk integration
can't raise change requests,

49
00:02:58.859 --> 00:03:00.188
which is the third gap.

50
00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:02.784
So a ServiceNow flow raises a standard,

51
00:03:02.784 --> 00:03:07.488
normal or emergency change from the
request, based on its change class.

52
00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:10.138
The kit plans that flow
for its second pass.

53
00:03:10.138 --> 00:03:14.787
Until then, the approver raises the
change record by hand and links it.

54
00:03:14.787 --> 00:03:17.750
Either way, closure still
waits for the re-read.

55
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.847
Disconnected applications, the
ones with only an admin console,

56
00:03:21.847 --> 00:03:24.088
run in the declared-exception lane.

57
00:03:24.088 --> 00:03:28.573
That means a service desk ticket, a
named approver before the session,

58
00:03:28.573 --> 00:03:32.508
a time-limited PIM activation,
a recorded session, an end date,

59
00:03:32.508 --> 00:03:36.600
and a plan-of-action item to
automate or retire the application.

60
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.450
The danger is the
exception that never ends.

61
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.800
End dates and reviews are what stop it.

62
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.380
The ServiceNow flow chooses
the change type from the class,

63
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.472
so a pre-authorized standard change,
a normal change with approvals,

64
00:03:49.472 --> 00:03:53.490
or an emergency change each
leave the record the class needs.

65
00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.621
On the federal side, access
restrictions for change call for

66
00:04:00.621 --> 00:04:04.003
the connector and spoke
identities, with their permissions.

67
00:04:04.003 --> 00:04:07.155
Access enforcement and
least privilege call for

68
00:04:07.155 --> 00:04:11.077
SailPoint's provisioning events
and PIM activation records.

69
00:04:11.077 --> 00:04:15.039
Change control calls for change
records linked from each request.

70
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:18.367
Session audit isn't in any
baseline, but it's added here,

71
00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:22.753
so declared-exception sessions are
recorded with counsel consulted.

72
00:04:22.753 --> 00:04:25.427
And each exception gets
a plan-of-action item.

73
00:04:25.427 --> 00:04:27.153
Validation is manual.

74
00:04:27.153 --> 00:04:32.139
Run one task per lane from start to
finish, and confirm each leaves its record.

75
00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:34.246
Then re-run the identity check,

76
00:04:34.246 --> 00:04:38.290
so no integration identity
holds more than its lane needs.

77
00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.877
PIM is available in Azure Government.

78
00:04:40.877 --> 00:04:46.042
The spoke's documentation shows
only the worldwide Graph endpoint,

79
00:04:46.042 --> 00:04:49.915
so it fits GCC, but confirm
GCC High with ServiceNow.

80
00:04:49.915 --> 00:04:52.303
To roll back, run the paired action.

81
00:04:52.303 --> 00:04:55.264
A leaver's deprovisioning
is never rolled back.

82
00:04:55.264 --> 00:04:57.850
They come back through a new request.

83
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.125
The re-read gate closes the first gap.

84
00:05:04.125 --> 00:05:08.537
Out of the box, SailPoint treats a
mapped ServiceNow status as done.

85
00:05:08.537 --> 00:05:11.014
So add an awaiting verification state,

86
00:05:11.014 --> 00:05:14.574
and a business rule that turns
any close into that state.

87
00:05:14.574 --> 00:05:17.172
The only exception is
an update that carries

88
00:05:17.172 --> 00:05:20.050
new evidence and comes
from the verifier account.

89
00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.225
A named exception approver
can still cancel with a note.

90
00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.499
Apply the same rule to the
incident and request tables,

91
00:05:26.499 --> 00:05:30.137
so SailPoint's status mapping
no longer decides completion.

92
00:05:30.137 --> 00:05:32.237
Then schedule the re-read client.

93
00:05:32.237 --> 00:05:36.151
It re-reads the account, the
identity, or the Graph user.

94
00:05:36.151 --> 00:05:39.855
If the target matches, it closes
the record with the query,

95
00:05:39.855 --> 00:05:42.062
the time and a hash of the response.

96
00:05:42.062 --> 00:05:45.713
If it doesn't match, it reopens
the record with the differences.

97
00:05:45.713 --> 00:05:48.878
A SailPoint read reflects
the last aggregation,

98
00:05:48.878 --> 00:05:52.676
so allow a grace period, tuned
to the aggregation schedule.

99
00:05:52.676 --> 00:05:55.180
The verifier account comes from gate one,

100
00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.310
and its audit trail is what
makes every closure attributable.

101
00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.922
The workbook's fifth chapter adds
two things to the re-read client.

102
00:06:06.922 --> 00:06:08.161
The first is a guard.

103
00:06:08.161 --> 00:06:11.000
A record whose intended
state names nothing

104
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.147
checkable used to close on
nothing, with evidence.

105
00:06:14.147 --> 00:06:16.560
Now it's skipped, and never verified.

106
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.635
The second is an offline mode.

107
00:06:18.635 --> 00:06:22.685
It makes the same decisions using
saved records and saved reads.

108
00:06:22.685 --> 00:06:24.443
It never writes anything,

109
00:06:24.443 --> 00:06:28.210
and it treats a missing read
as an error, never as a match.

110
00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.960
Six fictional records are re-read at noon.

111
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.123
Three verify.

112
00:06:32.123 --> 00:06:37.198
A terminated user who's still enabled is
a mismatch, so that record would reopen.

113
00:06:37.198 --> 00:06:40.273
One read is older than
the change, so it waits.

114
00:06:40.273 --> 00:06:41.998
And the empty one is skipped.

115
00:06:41.998 --> 00:06:44.706
A day later, the grace period has run out,

116
00:06:44.706 --> 00:06:47.585
so that stale read becomes a mismatch too.

117
00:06:47.585 --> 00:06:52.078
The business rule's own tests confirm
that evidence alone isn't enough,

118
00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:54.247
and neither is the verifier alone.

119
00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:59.124
Before this chapter, the client could only
run against a live instance and tenant,

120
00:06:59.124 --> 00:07:02.830
so its decisions were visible
only in mocked unit tests.

121
00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.527
On the federal side, change
control calls for the

122
00:07:09.527 --> 00:07:12.768
evidence attachment and hash
on every closed record.

123
00:07:12.768 --> 00:07:16.604
Control verification calls for
the re-read result and its time.

124
00:07:16.604 --> 00:07:21.868
Continuous monitoring calls for counts of
closed and reopened records each period.

125
00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:25.943
Audit record generation calls for
the verifier account's audit trail.

126
00:07:25.943 --> 00:07:30.224
To validate, the kit's tests prove
that a dry run writes nothing,

127
00:07:30.224 --> 00:07:35.093
that a match closes, that a mismatch
reopens, and that a stale read waits.

128
00:07:35.093 --> 00:07:39.536
On a live system, a person's close
should land in awaiting verification,

129
00:07:39.536 --> 00:07:43.367
and only the client should close
it, once the account matches.

130
00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:48.942
The re-read uses SailPoint's account and
search A P I's and Graph's users endpoint.

131
00:07:48.942 --> 00:07:52.081
GCC High uses its own Graph host.

132
00:07:52.081 --> 00:07:56.780
Until the client runs on a schedule,
the verifier runs it by hand once a day.

133
00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.110
To roll back, disable the business rule,

134
00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.530
though closures lose their
evidence while it's off.

135
00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:10.918
Once the loop is running, it follows the
Federal Modernization Workbook's tempo.

136
00:08:10.918 --> 00:08:12.380
A P1 halts work.

137
00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:17.180
P2 has one hour, P3 has a
day, and P4 has three days.

138
00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.305
These are the program's
windows, not federal ones.

139
00:08:20.305 --> 00:08:23.452
Schedule the drift reconciliation
and the re-read client,

140
00:08:23.452 --> 00:08:26.730
and keep their outputs with the
date and the checker version.

141
00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.703
Report five measures to the
governance board: detection coverage,

142
00:08:30.703 --> 00:08:33.447
how well windows are
kept, the escalation rate,

143
00:08:33.447 --> 00:08:38.142
the share of findings closed by machine,
and the number of declared exceptions.

144
00:08:38.142 --> 00:08:41.910
Together they show whether the
loop is closing on machines,

145
00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:44.755
inside its windows, or
leaning on exceptions.

146
00:08:44.755 --> 00:08:46.630
Export the evidence nightly.

147
00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.809
That's planned for the kit's second pass,

148
00:08:48.809 --> 00:08:51.805
and the workbook already
builds the ServiceNow half.

149
00:08:51.805 --> 00:08:54.810
Review each declared
exception at its end date,

150
00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.430
and each autonomous class every quarter.

151
00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.696
These measures are the workbook's design,
from its Ticket-to-Machine chapter,

152
00:09:01.696 --> 00:09:06.390
and they're what leadership reads to see
whether the loop is actually working.

153
00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:14.590
The tempo gate also routes the everyday
identity fixes through the loop.

154
00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.478
Most of this mapping is
the program's inference.

155
00:09:17.478 --> 00:09:23.182
A wrong fact in HR gets corrected in HR,
and SailPoint picks it up on its next read,

156
00:09:23.182 --> 00:09:26.078
since it can't write HR's attributes back.

157
00:09:26.078 --> 00:09:29.771
A late personnel action shows
up as a lifecycle-state change,

158
00:09:29.771 --> 00:09:33.390
and the leaver state disables
the account and its sessions.

159
00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.211
An urgent leaver that HR
hasn't entered yet runs

160
00:09:36.211 --> 00:09:39.465
the same leaver sequence
through an emergency change.

161
00:09:39.465 --> 00:09:43.465
A write made outside the pipeline is
caught by native change detection.

162
00:09:43.465 --> 00:09:47.815
SailPoint restores the value, and
the writer's access gets reviewed.

163
00:09:47.815 --> 00:09:52.253
An account that matches nobody gets
correlated to a person or disabled.

164
00:09:52.253 --> 00:09:56.315
A rule that grants the wrong access
is changed through a normal change.

165
00:09:56.315 --> 00:10:00.477
And a credential that outlives the
person is outside both products.

166
00:10:00.477 --> 00:10:04.220
It's handled by the card system
and the certificate platform.

167
00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:12.684
The workbook's seventh chapter reads
closed findings back out of ServiceNow,

168
00:10:12.684 --> 00:10:17.545
using a read-only role, and writes one of
the workbook's evidence records for each.

169
00:10:17.545 --> 00:10:21.388
Approval, executing identity
and what ran are left empty,

170
00:10:21.388 --> 00:10:25.395
each with its reason, because
the first pass doesn't actuate.

171
00:10:25.395 --> 00:10:27.620
Four checks run on every record.

172
00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:29.808
Does the evidence still match its hash?

173
00:10:29.808 --> 00:10:31.170
Is there evidence at all?

174
00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:32.958
Did the intended state match?

175
00:10:32.958 --> 00:10:34.871
And did it close inside its window?

176
00:10:34.871 --> 00:10:38.720
Five fictional records come out
with two of three closed on time.

177
00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.719
Three have problems: one closed late, one
had its evidence edited after closing,

178
00:10:43.719 --> 00:10:46.795
and one was closed with a
note that just says done.

179
00:10:46.795 --> 00:10:50.145
The workbook's design calls
that prose, not evidence.

180
00:10:50.145 --> 00:10:53.495
Each line carries the hash
of the line before it,

181
00:10:53.495 --> 00:10:56.845
so the ISSO's verifier
catches a removed record.

182
00:10:56.845 --> 00:10:59.920
The package can show tampering,
but it isn't signed.

183
00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.722
A later month continues the chain
from the previous month's head,

184
00:11:03.722 --> 00:11:06.880
so the packages link from
one period to the next.

185
00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.498
On the federal side,
continuous monitoring calls

186
00:11:13.498 --> 00:11:16.505
for the scheduled run outputs
and the five measures.

187
00:11:16.505 --> 00:11:20.305
Risk response calls for window
adherence and the overdue list.

188
00:11:20.305 --> 00:11:23.105
Plans of action call for
the exception register.

189
00:11:23.105 --> 00:11:27.092
Audit review calls for the monthly
evidence package and its review.

190
00:11:27.092 --> 00:11:31.670
To validate, the evidence export and
the drift comparison are automated,

191
00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.654
but the five measures aren't yet.

192
00:11:33.654 --> 00:11:37.959
The manual part is a month of
scheduled runs with their outputs kept,

193
00:11:37.959 --> 00:11:42.955
the measures reported once, and the first
monthly package accepted by the ISSO.

194
00:11:42.955 --> 00:11:46.130
This gate uses nothing
beyond the earlier gates.

195
00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:50.001
Until the export exists, build
the monthly package by hand,

196
00:11:50.001 --> 00:11:53.630
from a ServiceNow report and
a SailPoint event search.

197
00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:56.218
And forward Entra's
audit logs to the SIEM,

198
00:11:56.218 --> 00:11:59.042
since Entra only keeps
them for thirty days.

199
00:11:59.042 --> 00:12:01.292
To roll back, stop the schedules.

200
00:12:01.292 --> 00:12:02.860
The records stay.

201
00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.567
Every lane writes the same record,

202
00:12:08.567 --> 00:12:12.410
and this slide shows where each
field comes from on these products.

203
00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:17.848
Origin is the SailPoint trigger, the
reconciliation finding, or the catalog request.

204
00:12:17.848 --> 00:12:22.610
Owner and clock come from the ServiceNow
assignment, the severity and the due date.

205
00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:27.310
Approval is ServiceNow's for low-authority
tasks, SailPoint's for owner-governed

206
00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:31.660
access, and the authorizing official's
acceptance for autonomous classes.

207
00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.760
The executing identity is
the connector's or the

208
00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:38.248
spoke's service principal, or
a person's PIM activation.

209
00:12:38.248 --> 00:12:43.148
What ran is the provisioning event, the
workflow version, or the session recording.

210
00:12:43.148 --> 00:12:45.572
The result is the re-read evidence.

211
00:12:45.572 --> 00:12:49.573
And closure is the closed record,
with its hash and attachment.

212
00:12:49.573 --> 00:12:51.473
The mapping is the program's.

213
00:12:51.473 --> 00:12:54.206
The authorizing official and the assessors

214
00:12:54.206 --> 00:12:57.161
decide whether a control
is actually satisfied.

215
00:12:57.161 --> 00:13:03.123
Today the workbook's export fills origin,
owner, result and closure from ServiceNow.

216
00:13:03.123 --> 00:13:06.740
The other three wait for a
later pass that actuates.

217
00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.456
Before closing, here are
the dangers the guide names,

218
00:13:13.456 --> 00:13:15.802
each with the gate that guards against it.

219
00:13:15.802 --> 00:13:20.184
Closing by ticket status completes
SailPoint's task with nothing checked,

220
00:13:20.184 --> 00:13:23.577
so the re-read rule goes on
every table SailPoint writes to.

221
00:13:23.577 --> 00:13:26.134
With two approval systems for one class,

222
00:13:26.134 --> 00:13:30.552
both approve or neither does, so
there's one approval system per class.

223
00:13:30.552 --> 00:13:33.715
Two writers for one attribute
overwrite each other,

224
00:13:33.715 --> 00:13:36.803
which the attribute map
and the writer check catch.

225
00:13:36.803 --> 00:13:40.891
A vendor-cloud action nobody recorded
leaves the reviewed boundary,

226
00:13:40.891 --> 00:13:42.828
so it goes in the data-flow record.

227
00:13:42.828 --> 00:13:47.753
A feature outside the authorization needs
written confirmation before production.

228
00:13:47.753 --> 00:13:51.440
A connector's M F A setting
isn't a reset procedure.

229
00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.633
An integration identity with
broad write rights is a back door,

230
00:13:55.633 --> 00:13:58.778
so it gets certificates,
two owners, and a review.

231
00:13:58.778 --> 00:14:02.764
And an exception that never ends
needs an end date and a review.

232
00:14:02.764 --> 00:14:05.901
Most of these come down
to a decision made early,

233
00:14:05.901 --> 00:14:10.760
in gates one and two, and enforced
later by the re-read and the reviews.

234
00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:16.097
The guide is still a draft.

235
00:14:16.097 --> 00:14:21.353
To move it to proved, build gates three
to six in a ServiceNow developer instance

236
00:14:21.353 --> 00:14:25.972
and a SailPoint sandbox, run the kit's
lab items, and record the evidence.

237
00:14:25.972 --> 00:14:27.609
Some questions are still open.

238
00:14:27.609 --> 00:14:32.125
Is the full feature set available
in SailPoint's FedRAMP environment,

239
00:14:32.125 --> 00:14:33.760
and what is its A P I domain?

240
00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.198
Are the apps in ServiceNow's
government store?

241
00:14:36.198 --> 00:14:39.048
And does the Entra spoke work in GCC High?

242
00:14:39.048 --> 00:14:43.385
To build the code, use the developer
workbook, Building Ticket-to-Machine.

243
00:14:43.385 --> 00:14:47.222
It has nine chapters, eight
with labs that run on a laptop.

244
00:14:47.222 --> 00:14:49.153
To learn the material in order,

245
00:14:49.153 --> 00:14:53.785
use the learning path for Ticket-to-Machine
on ServiceNow and SailPoint.

246
00:14:53.785 --> 00:14:57.991
For the reasons behind it, see two
of the workbook's books: Governance,

247
00:14:57.991 --> 00:15:02.347
Drift and Evidence, and Compliance
Automation and Day-Two Operations.

248
00:15:02.347 --> 00:15:04.761
The loop, closed, looks like this.

249
00:15:04.761 --> 00:15:09.751
The ticket authorizes a machine, the
machine acts under a service identity,

250
00:15:09.751 --> 00:15:12.085
and only a re-read closes the ticket.

251
00:15:12.085 --> 00:15:16.136
The guide was rewritten as
a gated guide on November 1,

252
00:15:16.136 --> 00:15:19.690
2026, and that rewrite
hasn't been reviewed yet.
